Schedule member rules within model components

Represent rule schedules as trees so ordered strata can run inside
recursive model components. Infer local rule scopes before index
selection and bind their owner parameters before lowering to RAM.

Keep consumption checkpoints per model and static stratum. Publish
shared member subtrees and maintain one latest secondary index across
readers so separate consumers do not rebuild equivalent trees.

Close compatible rules within runtime morphism components, carrying
both primary and secondary trees onward after each component closes.
Place parent rule strata and child traversals in one dependency order
inside each parent component. Close parent/child and sibling feedback
at their common owner, while keeping ambient feedback global. Rebuild
child graphs on visits because outer transport can change topology.

Group independent dependency components at the same longest-path
level into one stratum so they share consumption checkpoints. Close
recursive levels before their consumers while retaining nested model
traversals and the outer allocation loop.

Prepare descendant closure before parent rules read child relations,
including secondary indices and children without local rules. Share
checkpoints at the bound owner prefix and aggregate graph preparation
for every nested traversal, including allocation-only bodies.

Discard scoped consumption checkpoints after model merges while
retaining the latest shared indices. Preserve interrupted closure and
the outer allocation loop. Keep simultaneous scheduling as default.

Test acyclic and cyclic transport, hierarchical rule ordering, parent
and sibling feedback, secondary indices, late morphisms, nested graph
changes, model merges, ambient fallback, and interrupted evaluation
in native and desugared evaluation modes.
